      "README.md": "# Mini Relational Tuple Filter & Projection Engine\n\n## Architectural Overview\n\nA relational tuple-at-a-time volcano iterator engine implementing Kleene three-valued logic (TRUE/FALSE/UNKNOWN), WHERE filtering, SELECT projection with expressions, ORDER BY NULLS LAST, and LIMIT/OFFSET slicing.\n\nIn production relational and analytical database systems—spanning PostgreSQL, SQLite, MySQL/InnoDB, and DuckDB—understanding how query clauses, schema constraints, index structures, and storage engines execute under the hood separates surface-level query authors from staff-level database engineers. This capstone build lab guides you through implementing **Mini Relational Tuple Filter & Projection Engine** from first principles with deterministic self-test invariants.\n\n## Guided Implementation Walkthrough (4 Progressive Steps)\n\n### Step 1: Kleene Three-Valued Logic (3VL) Evaluator\n\n- **Core Database Concept**: Evaluating NULL Comparisons as UNKNOWN and Propagating Through AND / OR / NOT\n- **Target Module**: `index.js` (`RelationalQueryEngine.eval3VL`)\n- **Engineering Specification**: Implement `evalComparison(left, op, right)` and `combine3VL(op, a, b)` using Kleene three-valued logic (`\"TRUE\"`, `\"FALSE\"`, `\"UNKNOWN\"`). Any standard comparison (`=`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`) where either operand is `null` or `undefined` must return `\"UNKNOWN\"`, whereas `IS DISTINCT FROM` treats `null` as a comparable value.\n- **Implementation Hint**: `Map TRUE->1, UNKNOWN->0, FALSE->-1: AND is Math.min(a,b), OR is Math.max(a,b), NOT is -a.`\n- **Verification Invariant**: deterministic assertion verified in `test.js` step 1\n\n### Step 2: WHERE Predicate Filtering (Coercing UNKNOWN to FALSE)\n\n- **Core Database Concept**: Filtering Relation Tuples Strictly on Logical TRUE\n- **Target Module**: `index.js` (`RelationalQueryEngine.filterWhere`)\n- **Engineering Specification**: Implement `filterWhere(rows, predicateFn)` to evaluate a 3VL predicate across every input tuple and retain **only** rows that evaluate to `\"TRUE\"` (or boolean `true`), discarding both `\"FALSE\"` and `\"UNKNOWN\"` rows just as SQL `WHERE` clauses do.\n- **Implementation Hint**: `Keep only rows where predicateFn(row) === \"TRUE\" or predicateFn(row) === true.`\n- **Verification Invariant**: deterministic assertion verified in `test.js` step 2\n\n### Step 3: SELECT Projection & Derived Column Aliases\n\n- **Core Database Concept**: Relational Projection π and Computed Tuple Attributes\n- **Target Module**: `index.js` (`RelationalQueryEngine.projectSelect`)\n- **Engineering Specification**: Implement `projectSelect(rows, projections, distinct = false)` to transform each input tuple into a projected tuple containing only the requested column names or computed expressions, optionally deduplicating tuples using structural serialization.\n- **Implementation Hint**: `Map each specification { alias, expr } to outRow[alias] = typeof expr === \"function\" ? expr(row) : row[expr].`\n- **Verification Invariant**: deterministic assertion verified in `test.js` step 3\n\n### Step 4: Multi-Key ORDER BY (NULLS LAST) & LIMIT / OFFSET Slicing\n\n- **Core Database Concept**: Deterministic Tuple Ordering and Cursor Window Slicing\n- **Target Module**: `index.js` (`RelationalQueryEngine.orderByAndSlice`)\n- **Engineering Specification**: Implement `orderByAndSlice(rows, orderSpecs, limit, offset)` to sort projected tuples across multiple `{ col, dir, nullsLast }` keys without mutating the source relation, then apply `offset` and `limit` window bounds.\n- **Implementation Hint**: `Check nullness before comparing values; respect nullsLast flag, then slice(offset, offset + limit).`\n- **Verification Invariant**: deterministic assertion verified in `test.js` step 4\n\n## Relational & Storage Engine Guarantees\n\n- **Curriculum Domain**: Relational Foundations\n- **Difficulty & Time Budget**: Beginner (~30 minutes)\n- **Key Architectural Highlights**:\n  - Kleene 3-valued truth table evaluation (TRUE, FALSE, UNKNOWN) for NULL-safe predicates\n  - Logical execution order pipeline: FROM -> WHERE -> SELECT -> DISTINCT -> ORDER BY -> LIMIT/OFFSET\n  - Deterministic multi-key sorting with configurable NULLS FIRST / NULLS LAST ordering\n- **Core Primitives & Tags**: `Relational Algebra`, `Three-Valued Logic`, `Tuple Iterator`, `Query Pipeline`, `Projection`\n\n## Related Curriculum Units\n\n- [why databases exist](/topic/why-databases-exist/)\n- [tables rows columns types](/topic/tables-rows-columns-types/)\n- [select projection aliases](/topic/select-projection-aliases/)\n- [where filtering predicates](/topic/where-filtering-predicates/)\n- [boolean logic three valued null](/topic/boolean-logic-three-valued-null/)\n\n## Production Database Context & Verification\n\nThis from-scratch relational engine component is engineered with zero external dependencies and deterministic verification harnesses. Every internal database invariant—from three-valued logic (`TRUE` / `FALSE` / `UNKNOWN`), referential integrity, and fan-out-free join pre-aggregation to B+Tree page splits, MVCC snapshot visibility (`xmin` / `xmax`), slotted-page compaction, and ARIES write-ahead log crash recovery—is asserted directly in `test.js`. Use the interactive browser workbench above to complete the 4 guided steps in **Guided Lab** mode or inspect and benchmark the complete reference architecture in **Full Solution** mode.\n"
