      "README.md": "# B+Tree Page Simulator & Automated Workload Index Advisor\n\n## Architectural Overview\n\nAn in-memory B+Tree index simulator with doubly linked leaf sibling pointers for range scans and an automated workload index advisor applying the Equality -> Sort -> Range + INCLUDE covering rule.\n\nIn production relational and analytical database systems—spanning PostgreSQL, SQLite, MySQL/InnoDB, and DuckDB—understanding how query clauses, schema constraints, index structures, and storage engines execute under the hood separates surface-level query authors from staff-level database engineers. This capstone build lab guides you through implementing **B+Tree Page Simulator & Automated Workload Index Advisor** from first principles with deterministic self-test invariants.\n\n## Guided Implementation Walkthrough (4 Progressive Steps)\n\n### Step 1: B+Tree Sorted Leaf Insertion & Midpoint Split\n\n- **Core Database Concept**: Splitting Overflowing Leaf Pages and Linking Sibling Pointers\n- **Target Module**: `index.js` (`BPlusTreeIndexAdvisor.insertKey`)\n- **Engineering Specification**: Implement `insertKey(key, rowId)` to insert into the appropriate sorted leaf page and split any leaf exceeding `this.maxKeys`, linking `leftLeaf.next = rightLeaf`.\n- **Implementation Hint**: `Insert { key, rowId } in sorted order; when leaf exceeds maxKeys, split at mid and link left.next = right.`\n- **Verification Invariant**: deterministic assertion verified in `test.js` step 1\n\n### Step 2: Leaf-Chain Range Scan Traversal\n\n- **Core Database Concept**: Point-Seek to Lower Bound Followed by Sequential Leaf Pointer Walk\n- **Target Module**: `index.js` (`BPlusTreeIndexAdvisor.rangeScan`)\n- **Engineering Specification**: Implement `rangeScan(minKey, maxKey)` to find the starting leaf and traverse sibling `next` pointers, returning `{ rowIds, pagesRead }`.\n- **Implementation Hint**: `Locate the first leaf capable of holding minKey, then walk leaf.next until key > maxKey.`\n- **Verification Invariant**: deterministic assertion verified in `test.js` step 2\n\n### Step 3: Leftmost Prefix Utilization Analyzer\n\n- **Core Database Concept**: Why Range Predicates Stop Subsequent Index Key Columns From Narrowing the Scan\n- **Target Module**: `index.js` (`BPlusTreeIndexAdvisor.matchedPrefixCount`)\n- **Engineering Specification**: Implement `matchedPrefixCount(indexCols, equalityCols, rangeCols)` to compute how many leading columns of `indexCols` can be used as B+Tree search bounds.\n- **Implementation Hint**: `Walk indexCols in order: increment count on equality; if range col encountered, increment once and break.`\n- **Verification Invariant**: deterministic assertion verified in `test.js` step 3\n\n### Step 4: Automated Equality -> Sort -> Range + INCLUDE Index Advisor\n\n- **Core Database Concept**: Synthesizing an Optimal Covering Index for a Parameterized Query Shape\n- **Target Module**: `index.js` (`BPlusTreeIndexAdvisor.recommendIndex`)\n- **Engineering Specification**: Implement `recommendIndex({ table, equalityCols, rangeCols, orderCols, selectCols })` to synthesize `{ keyCols, includeCols, ddl }` following the ESR + covering index design pattern.\n- **Implementation Hint**: `Order equalityCols by selectivity, then orderCols (if no range before sort), then rangeCols, and put remaining selectCols in includeCols.`\n- **Verification Invariant**: deterministic assertion verified in `test.js` step 4\n\n## Relational & Storage Engine Guarantees\n\n- **Curriculum Domain**: B+Trees & Indexing Architecture\n- **Difficulty & Time Budget**: Advanced (~45 minutes)\n- **Key Architectural Highlights**:\n  - B+Tree leaf node splits with upward separator key promotion and sibling leaf pointer linking\n  - O(log_B N + K) range scan traversal walking leaf next pointers without re-descending internal nodes\n  - Automated composite index synthesis ordering Equality -> ORDER BY -> Range columns with INCLUDE payload\n- **Core Primitives & Tags**: `B+Tree`, `Composite Index`, `ESR Rule`, `Covering Index`, `Range Scan`\n\n## Related Curriculum Units\n\n- [why indexes work io cost model](/topic/why-indexes-work-io-cost-model/)\n- [btree bplustree internal anatomy](/topic/btree-bplustree-internal-anatomy/)\n- [clustered vs secondary indexes](/topic/clustered-vs-secondary-indexes/)\n- [composite indexes leftmost prefix](/topic/composite-indexes-leftmost-prefix/)\n- [covering indexes index only scans](/topic/covering-indexes-index-only-scans/)\n\n## Production Database Context & Verification\n\nThis from-scratch relational engine component is engineered with zero external dependencies and deterministic verification harnesses. Every internal database invariant—from three-valued logic (`TRUE` / `FALSE` / `UNKNOWN`), referential integrity, and fan-out-free join pre-aggregation to B+Tree page splits, MVCC snapshot visibility (`xmin` / `xmax`), slotted-page compaction, and ARIES write-ahead log crash recovery—is asserted directly in `test.js`. Use the interactive browser workbench above to complete the 4 guided steps in **Guided Lab** mode or inspect and benchmark the complete reference architecture in **Full Solution** mode.\n"
